# EVERY SUBJECT EVERY POPULATION ACTUALLY PRODUCES. One row per (contract, subject), against
# which `algebra/declared_subjects.sqlc` is the difference every contract in this repository
# rests on.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THE OTHER OPERAND, AND THE TWO ARE NOT SYMMETRIC IN WHAT THEY PROVE. A subject declared
#    and not produced is a contract naming something that does not exist. A subject produced and
#    not declared is the tree growing past its own roster. Both are findings and they are
#    different findings, which is why `reports/integrity.sqlc` takes the difference BOTH ways and
#    why neither direction may be dropped as the obvious one.
#
# ⛔ `checks/all.sqlc` AND NOT `reports/violations.sqlc`, and the distinction is the whole reason
#   a roster is joined first. A check that filters its population disappears from a `GROUP BY`
#   when the population is empty, so the single most important verdict, ⛔ VACUOUS, is exactly
#   the one a grouping cannot produce. `all` carries a row per declared rule whatever happened;
#   `violations` carries only the failures. Differencing against the wrong one would report every
#   passing rule as undeclared.
#
# ⚠️ `diagrams/catalogue.sqlc` IS THE PRODUCED SIDE FOR TABLES AND IT IS NOT DERIVED FROM THE
#   ROSTER. It reads `information_schema`, which is the only honest source for which tables
#   actually exist: a contract needs a population it did not write. That is also why
#   `examples/compositions/main.rs`'s table predicate had to learn that *reads a base table* is not
#   *reads `pm.`*, and the same predicate has since had to learn `public.` for the compose DAG.
-- the five populations, each labelled with the contract it answers to.
SELECT 'rules' AS contract, c.rule AS subject
FROM ( :compose(checks/all.sqlc) ) c
UNION ALL
SELECT 'arithmetic', z.site
FROM ( :compose(arithmetic/all.sqlc) ) z
UNION ALL
SELECT 'algebra', w.law
FROM ( :compose(algebra/all.sqlc) ) w
UNION ALL
SELECT 'diagrams', k.object
FROM ( :compose(diagrams/catalogue.sqlc) ) k
UNION ALL
SELECT 'diagram laws', e.slug
FROM ( :compose(diagrams/expected.sqlc) ) e
